ur Lean R, two Tossup", "Polymarket prices Green at 68% to win", "Soto trails in cash on hand ($250K to $450K) despite higher total fundraising; the only polls are Democratic-sponsored and show ties"], "rationale": "The redrawn district's Republican lean, a ratings consensus expecting a flip, and markets pricing Green near 68% outweigh Soto's incumbency and fundraising edge; the only public polls, both Democratic-sponsored, show a dead heat rather than a Soto lead, so the race sits clearly on the Republican side of tossup without reaching Likely R.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a late Democratic turnout surge breaking toward Soto would pull the race back toward a pure tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darren Soto (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Darren Soto, a Democratic incumbent, faces Republican Dan Green in a central Florida seat that redistricting reshaped into Republican-leaning territory. Outside ratings mostly call the race for Green, with four outlets rating it Likely Republican and four Lean Republican, while two call it a Tossup. Prediction markets give Soto roughly a one-in-three chance. The only public polls, conducted by Democratic sponsors, show the two tied in October after an August survey also found a dead heat.\n\nSoto has raised more money overall, about $3.7 million to Green's $2.7 million, but enters the final stretch with less cash on hand, $250,000 to Green's $450,000. Neither side has drawn meaningful outside spending. Soto has secured local endorsements, including Sheriff John Mina, while Green has backing from state agriculture leaders. The district's new Republican lean and the weight of expert ratings make Green the modest favorite, though the tight polling leaves real room for an upset.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3442, "margin": -2.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2846, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2806, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.37